    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 123,
      "bottomRightX": 552,
      "bottomRightY": 173,
      "content": "induces a map $h^* : T \\to \\{0, 1\\}$ from a subset $T$ of the unit sphere $S$ into $\\{0, 1\\}$ such that for any three mutually orthogonal points in $T$ exactly one is mapped by $h^*$ into 1.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 174,
      "bottomRightX": 553,
      "bottomRightY": 260,
      "content": "It will be convenient in what follows to represent points on $S$ by the vertices of a graph. Two vertices which are joined by an edge in the graph represent orthogonal points on $S$. When we say that a graph $\\Gamma$ is realizable on $S$ we mean that there is an assignment of points of $S$ to the vertices of $\\Gamma$, distinct points for distinct vertices, with the orthogonality relations as indicated in $\\Gamma$.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 90,
      "topLeftY": 268,
      "bottomRightX": 409,
      "bottomRightY": 285,
      "content": "Lemma 1. The following graph $\\Gamma_1$ is realizable on $S$.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 223,
      "topLeftY": 295,
      "bottomRightX": 409,
      "bottomRightY": 452,
      "content": "![img-0.jpeg](img-0.jpeg)",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "img-0.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 465,
      "bottomRightX": 552,
      "bottomRightY": 518,
      "content": "In fact, if $p$ and $q$ are points on $S$ such that $0 \\leq \\sin \\theta \\leq \\frac{1}{2}$ where $\\theta$ is the angle subtended by $p$ and $q$ at the center of $S$, then there exists a map $u : \\Gamma_1 \\to S$ such that $u(a_0) = p$ and $u(a_q) = q$.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 524,
      "bottomRightX": 553,
      "bottomRightY": 593,
      "content": "Proof. Since $u(a_8)$ is orthogonal to $u(a_0) \\cup u(a_9)$ and $u(a_7)$ is orthogonal to $u(a_8)$, $u(a_7)$ lies in the plane $u(a_0) \\cup u(a_9)$. Also since $u(a_7)$ is orthogonal to $u(a_8)$, we have that $\\varphi = \\pi/2 - \\theta$, where $\\varphi$ is the angle subtended at the center of $S$ by $u(a_0)$ and $u(a_7)$. Let $u(a_3) = \\bar{\\imath}$ and $u(a_8) = \\bar{k}$. Then we may take",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 117,
      "topLeftY": 600,
      "bottomRightX": 509,
      "bottomRightY": 619,
      "content": "$$u(a_1) = (\\bar{j} + x\\bar{k})(1 + x^2)^{-1/2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad u(a_2) = (\\bar{\\imath} + y\\bar{j})(1 + y^2)^{-1/2}.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 625,
      "bottomRightX": 319,
      "bottomRightY": 642,
      "content": "The orthogonality conditions then force",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 227,
      "topLeftY": 649,
      "bottomRightX": 406,
      "bottomRightY": 670,
      "content": "$$u(a_3) = (x\\bar{j} - \\bar{k})(1 + x^2)^{-1/2},$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 228,
      "topLeftY": 671,
      "bottomRightX": 404,
      "bottomRightY": 693,
      "content": "$$u(a_4) = (y\\bar{\\imath} - \\bar{j})(1 + y^2)^{-1/2},$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 690,
      "bottomRightX": 144,
      "bottomRightY": 706,
      "content": "and hence,",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 184,
      "topLeftY": 705,
      "bottomRightX": 448,
      "bottomRightY": 728,
      "content": "$$u(a_0) = (xy\\bar{\\imath} - x\\bar{j} + \\bar{k})(1 + x^2 + x^2y^2)^{-1/2},$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 185,
      "topLeftY": 730,
      "bottomRightX": 448,
